(function () {
  window.CCA = window.CCA || {};
  window.CCA.lessonsNew = window.CCA.lessonsNew || [];
  window.CCA.scenarios = window.CCA.scenarios || {};
  window.CCA.why = window.CCA.why || {};
  window.CCA.questions = window.CCA.questions || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }

  window.CCA.lessonsNew.push(
    {"id": "design-1", "n": 1, "domain": "design", "title": "Turning a business problem into a Claude solution", "body": "Architects rarely receive a technical problem. They receive an ambition such as \"improve customer service\". The first job is to turn it into a task, an outcome and a measure of success, and only then to ask whether Claude is the right tool. A good candidate task involves unstructured language, tolerates some variability, and has a cheap way to check or review the result.\n\nClaude is a poor fit where the answer must be exactly the same every time, where a simple rule or database query already works, or where a calculation must be exact. In those cases the sound design keeps the deterministic part in code and lets Claude handle the language part, for example by calling a calculation tool and explaining the result.\n\nBefore committing to a build, run a small prototype on representative real samples and score it against a rubric. This tests feasibility with evidence rather than opinion, and the samples become the seed of the evaluation set. In regulated decisions, frame Claude as a drafter or summariser with a named human decision owner, and involve Legal, Compliance and the Data Privacy Officer early.", "points": ["Convert a vague ambition into one concrete task, one outcome and one measure, and capture a baseline before building anything.", "Good fits involve unstructured language, tolerate variability and allow cheap review; poor fits need exact, repeatable or millisecond answers.", "Keep exact calculation, validation and policy thresholds in deterministic code, and let Claude call them as tools.", "Prototype on representative real samples with a rubric to test feasibility and to seed the evaluation set.", "In regulated or high-impact decisions, position Claude as an assistant to a human decision owner and involve Legal, Compliance and the DPO."], "traps": ["Choosing the model, framework or architecture before the task and success measure are agreed.", "Asking the model to do arithmetic or rule checks that ordinary code does exactly and cheaply.", "Judging feasibility from a few hand-picked demo prompts instead of a representative sample.", "Treating a regulated decision as fully automatable because the pilot looked accurate."], "sources": ["https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"]},
    {"id": "design-2", "n": 2, "domain": "design", "title": "End-to-end architecture: input, processing, output and feedback", "body": "An end-to-end Claude solution is more than a model call. The input stage receives, validates and normalises data: file types, sizes, language, and routing of malformed items to a manual path. The processing stage holds the prompts, retrieval, tools and any workflow logic. The output stage turns model text into something a downstream system can safely use. The feedback stage records what happened and learns from it.\n\nThe Messages API is stateless, so any memory, history or case state belongs in your application. For outputs, use a defined schema and validate values in code before anything is written to a system of record; a preventive check before the action is stronger than a detective check afterwards. Multi-step pipelines need idempotent steps, retries with backoff, and a manual-review or dead-letter path, so one failure does not restart the whole case.\n\nThe feedback loop is what keeps the system improving. Log inputs, outputs, prompt and model versions and a trace identifier across stages. Capture human corrections and feed sampled cases into the evaluation set. Without these, regressions are invisible and the team cannot tell which stage degraded.", "points": ["Design four stages explicitly: input validation, processing, output handling and a feedback loop.", "The API is stateless, so conversation and case state must be stored and supplied by the application.", "Validate structured output in code before it triggers any action; prefer preventive to detective controls.", "Make pipeline steps idempotent, retry transient failures and provide a manual or dead-letter route.", "Log versions and a trace ID per stage, and turn human corrections into evaluation cases."], "traps": ["Building input to output with no feedback path, so corrections never improve the system.", "Assuming the model remembers earlier requests when the application did not send the history.", "Relying on after-the-fact monitoring for something a validation gate could have blocked.", "Restarting a whole multi-step case after one failed step instead of retrying that step."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/working-with-messages", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs", "https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents"]},
    {"id": "design-3", "n": 3, "domain": "design", "title": "Choosing between augmented LLM, workflow and agent", "body": "Anthropic's guidance separates two families. Workflows are systems where LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths. Agents are systems where the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use. The building block under both is the augmented LLM: a model with retrieval, tools and memory. Many applications need nothing more than a well-prompted single call with retrieved context.\n\nThe guiding principle is to use the simplest design that meets the requirement, and add complexity only when measured results justify it. Workflows give predictability, easier testing and bounded cost, and suit tasks whose steps are known in advance. Agents suit open-ended problems where the number and order of steps cannot be predicted, and they pay for that flexibility with higher latency, variable cost and errors that can compound.\n\nAn agent also needs guardrails: clear stopping conditions or a step limit, well-documented tools, and human checkpoints for consequential actions. A common and sound hybrid is a workflow that handles the predictable bulk and escalates the hard minority of cases to an agent.", "points": ["Workflows follow predefined code paths; agents let the model direct its own steps and tool use.", "Start with a single augmented call and add structure only when evaluation shows it is needed.", "Choose workflows when steps are known and predictability, testability and cost control matter.", "Choose agents when the path cannot be predicted and the task justifies extra latency and cost.", "Give agents step limits, stopping conditions, good tool descriptions and human checkpoints for risky actions."], "traps": ["Picking an agent for a fixed process because agents seem more advanced.", "Skipping the simple single-call baseline and building a framework first.", "Letting an agent loop run without a step limit or a budget.", "Treating the choice as binary when a workflow with an agent for hard cases fits better."], "sources": ["https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview"]},
    {"id": "design-4", "n": 4, "domain": "design", "title": "Workflow patterns in practice: chaining, routing, parallelization and evaluator-optimizer", "body": "Four workflow patterns cover most predictable designs. Prompt chaining splits a task into fixed sequential steps, with optional programmatic checks (gates) between them; it trades some latency for accuracy because each call has a simpler job. Routing classifies an input and sends it to a specialised path, for example easy queries to a smaller model and hard ones to a more capable one, which improves both cost and focus.\n\nParallelization runs work concurrently in two forms. Sectioning splits a task into independent subtasks, such as separate compliance, privacy and tone checks on one document. Voting runs the same task several times and aggregates the answers where confidence matters. The evaluator-optimizer pattern has one call generate and another critique, looping until criteria are met; it only pays off when there are clear criteria and the iteration measurably improves the result.\n\nPick the pattern from the shape of the problem, not from habit. A pattern that adds calls without a measured gain only adds cost and latency. Combine patterns when needed: route first, then chain within each route, and run independent checks in parallel.", "points": ["Prompt chaining suits tasks that decompose cleanly into fixed steps, with gates between steps to catch errors early.", "Routing classifies inputs and directs them to specialised prompts, tools or models.", "Sectioning runs independent subtasks in parallel; voting repeats one task for higher confidence.", "Evaluator-optimizer needs clear criteria and a measurable gain from each round of feedback.", "Patterns compose: route, then chain, with independent checks run in parallel."], "traps": ["Using an evaluator loop when criteria are subjective or first-attempt quality already meets the bar.", "Running dependent steps in parallel, so a later step lacks the input it needs.", "Routing without measuring classifier accuracy, so misrouted cases fail silently.", "Adding voting everywhere, which multiplies cost without targeting the uncertain cases."], "sources": ["https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents"]},
    {"id": "design-5", "n": 5, "domain": "design", "title": "Multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies", "body": "In the orchestrator-worker design, a lead agent analyses the request, decomposes it into subtasks and delegates them to subagents, then synthesises their results. Anthropic's multi-agent research system uses this shape: subagents explore different directions in parallel, each in its own context window, and return condensed findings to the lead. This helps with breadth-first work that exceeds one context window and that splits into independent paths.\n\nContext isolation is central. A subagent does not see the parent conversation; the prompt the orchestrator writes is the main channel into it, and typically only its final message comes back. So the delegation brief must state the objective, the expected output format, the tools and sources to use, and clear task boundaries. Vague briefs lead to duplicated or missing work.\n\nMulti-agent systems cost many times more tokens than a single chat, so they are justified only for high-value tasks. They are a poor fit when steps depend tightly on shared context, as in many single-codebase editing tasks. Scale effort to query complexity, give subagents least-privilege tools, persist the plan externally, and design for partial failure.", "points": ["Orchestrator-worker: a lead agent decomposes, delegates to parallel subagents and synthesises results.", "Subagents have isolated contexts, so the delegation prompt must carry objective, output format, tools and boundaries.", "Subagents should return condensed findings, not full transcripts, to protect the lead's context.", "Multi-agent systems use far more tokens, so reserve them for high-value, parallelisable work.", "Scale effort to complexity, restrict each subagent's tools, persist the plan and handle partial failure."], "traps": ["Giving a subagent a one-line task and expecting it to know the wider goal.", "Using many agents for tightly coupled work where shared context matters.", "Applying the same heavy multi-agent run to simple questions.", "Letting one failed or slow subagent block or crash the whole run."], "sources": ["https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system", "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents", "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents"]},
    {"id": "design-6", "n": 6, "domain": "design", "title": "Decomposition techniques for complex problems", "body": "Decomposition makes a hard task tractable. Split by stage when a task has natural phases such as extract, validate, decide and draft. Split by independence when subtasks do not depend on each other, so they can run in parallel. Split by data when a large corpus must be processed in pieces, using a map-then-reduce shape: summarise or analyse each chunk, then combine the partial results.\n\nStatic decomposition, such as a prompt chain, fits when the subtasks are known in advance. Dynamic decomposition, as in orchestrator-workers, fits when the subtasks depend on the input and cannot be listed beforehand. Draw the dependency graph first: run independent branches together and gate dependent steps on validated upstream output.\n\nGranularity is a trade-off. One giant prompt that extracts, judges and writes tends to be inconsistent and hard to debug. But splitting too finely adds latency and cost and gives errors more chances to propagate. A good boundary is where a step can be tested on its own with its own evaluation cases. That also makes diagnosis easier: test each stage separately to find where quality is lost.", "points": ["Decompose by stage, by independence or by data, depending on the shape of the task.", "Use static chains when subtasks are known and dynamic orchestration when they depend on the input.", "Map the dependency graph, parallelise independent branches and gate dependent steps.", "Choose granularity so each step is independently testable; too coarse and too fine both hurt.", "Diagnose failures by evaluating each stage on its own, because errors compound across steps."], "traps": ["Putting extraction, judgement and drafting in one prompt and then struggling to locate failures.", "Splitting into many tiny steps whose latency and error propagation outweigh the benefit.", "Parallelising steps that actually depend on earlier results.", "Evaluating only the final output so that a weak middle stage stays hidden."], "sources": ["https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system"]},
    {"id": "design-7", "n": 7, "domain": "design", "title": "Aligning solutions to business value pillars", "body": "A solution earns its place by moving a business measure. The pillars in the blueprint are efficiency, transformation, productivity, cost and performance SLAs. Efficiency is less effort or time per unit of work, such as handling time per case. Productivity is more output per person. Transformation is enabling something previously uneconomic or impossible. Cost covers the full cost of running the solution, and performance SLAs cover latency, throughput and availability commitments.\n\nTranslate each pillar into a metric with a baseline, then test design options against it. Routing simple work to a smaller model, using asynchronous batch processing for non-urgent bulk work, and keeping humans only where they add value all trade quality, speed and cost differently. The Message Batches API is asynchronous and suited to work no one is waiting on; it is not the right tool for an interactive latency commitment.\n\nA credible business case counts the total cost, including human review, evaluation, monitoring and maintenance, and compares it with a measured baseline. Stakeholders asking for the highest accuracy at the lowest cost and latency need a measured trade-off from a prototype, not a promise. Prioritise use cases by value and feasibility, and mark quick wins separately from transformational bets.", "points": ["Map each use case to a pillar and a measurable metric with a baseline.", "Efficiency and productivity are measured in work outcomes, not model benchmark scores.", "Use routing and batch processing to manage cost, and keep interactive SLAs on synchronous paths.", "Include review, evaluation, monitoring and maintenance in the total cost of the business case.", "Present quality, latency and cost trade-offs from measured prototype data and prioritise by value and feasibility."], "traps": ["Quoting model benchmarks as evidence of business value.", "Counting only token cost and ignoring human review and operations.", "Using asynchronous batch processing for a path with a tight interactive latency target.", "Promising maximum quality, minimum cost and minimum latency together without measured trade-offs."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing", "https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents"]}
  );

  window.CCA.scenarios.scDS1 = {"title": "Claims intake for a regional insurer", "text": "A regional insurer receives tens of thousands of claim submissions each week by email and web portal: scanned PDF forms, photos and free-text descriptions. Staff currently key the data into the claims system, check policy cover, and flag suspected fraud. The steps are fixed by internal policy: extract data, validate against the policy, classify severity, and draft a decision letter. Payouts above a set threshold need an adjuster's approval, and the business must be able to explain each decision afterwards. Goals are shorter handling time, low cost per claim and a full audit trail. There is no labelled evaluation set yet, and the Legal team has asked to be involved in any automated decisioning."};
  window.CCA.scenarios.scDS2 = {"title": "Market intelligence research platform", "text": "A consultancy wants a platform that produces market intelligence reports for its analysts. A typical request, such as mapping competitors in a new sector, needs many web and document sources searched from different angles, then merged into a cited report. Analysts currently spend several days on each report, and the reports are high value to clients. Requests vary: some are simple fact checks, while others are broad investigations. Management is concerned about runaway token spend and wants every claim traceable to a source."};

  window.CCA.questions.push(
    S("ds001", "design", "A COO asks for \"an AI assistant to improve customer service\". Which first step best turns this ambition into a buildable Claude solution?", ["Pick the most capable model available and start a pilot with a small group of support agents", "Write a broad system prompt and ask agents to report back on which replies feel most useful to them", "Name one concrete task and outcome, such as billing first-contact resolution, and agree how to measure it", "Select an agent framework first so that the design can grow with later requests from other teams"], 2, "A vague ambition must become a concrete task, outcome and measure before model or framework choices mean anything.", null),
    S("ds002", "design", "A pricing feature must compute customer quotes from rate tables and tax rules exactly. Which design is most sound?", ["Have Claude call a calculation tool, then explain the result in plain language", "Ask Claude to compute the quote itself, supported by worked examples in the prompt", "Run each quote through Claude three times and keep the most frequent total", "Move to the largest available model so that the arithmetic becomes dependable"], 0, "Exact calculation belongs in deterministic code. Claude adds value by calling it and explaining the outcome.", null),
    S("ds003", "design", "For the claims process, which step is the strongest candidate for Claude rather than conventional code?", ["Applying the payout approval threshold to each claim", "Extracting data from scanned forms and free text", "Checking that a policy number matches a record in the system", "Routing claims over the payout limit to an adjuster queue"], 1, "Extraction from unstructured, variable documents is where language models add most value. The other steps are exact rules that code does cheaply and repeatably.", "scDS1"),
    M("ds004", "design", 2, "In the claims workflow, which TWO steps should remain deterministic code or a human decision rather than free model judgement?", ["Summarising a long claimant narrative for the adjuster", "Applying the payout threshold that triggers approval", "Drafting a first version of the decision letter", "Giving final approval on payouts above the threshold", "Extracting the date of loss from a photographed form"], [1, 3], "Policy thresholds are exact rules, and above-threshold approval is a named human accountability. Summarising, drafting and extraction suit Claude with review.", "scDS1"),
    S("ds005", "design", "A department asks for a chatbot because staff cannot find the right HR policy document. Which framing best fits the actual problem?", ["Train a bespoke model on all HR documents so that staff never need to search", "Build a general-purpose agent with web access to look up policy questions", "Ask Claude to answer from memory and add a disclaimer to every reply", "Ground answers in the policy library by retrieval, with citations to the source document"], 3, "The problem is finding and applying specific documents, which retrieval with citations addresses directly and verifiably.", null),
    S("ds006", "design", "A sponsor wants to commit budget after one impressive demo. What should an architect propose?", ["Score a prototype on real, representative cases against an agreed rubric first", "Approve the full budget, since a convincing demo shows the technology is capable of the work", "Wait for a newer model release so that the feasibility question answers itself", "Ask the vendor for a statement that the use case will work"], 0, "Evidence on representative data tests feasibility, and the samples seed the evaluation set.", null),
    S("ds007", "design", "A bank wants Claude to approve or decline consumer credit applications automatically. Which response best reflects sound architecture?", ["Automate fully, because accuracy in the pilot looked high enough to rely on", "Keep a human as decision owner and involve Legal and Compliance early", "Let Claude decide and add a monthly manual audit of declined applications", "Use two Claude calls to vote and treat their agreement as the final decision"], 1, "A regulated, high-impact decision needs a named human owner and early Legal and Compliance input; later audits or voting do not replace that.", null),
    S("ds008", "design", "Which requirement would make Claude a poor choice for the core of a component?", ["Reading free-text complaints to identify the main topic of each one", "Producing a first draft of a reply for human review", "Returning the identical checksum result for an input every time", "Summarising a long thread of messages for a handover"], 2, "Exact, repeatable results are a job for deterministic code. The other tasks are language work where variation is acceptable.", null),
    M("ds009", "design", 2, "Which TWO characteristics make a task a good candidate for a Claude-based solution?", ["Input is unstructured language such as emails or documents", "Results must be bit-for-bit identical on every run", "Outputs can be reviewed or checked cheaply before use", "An existing rule or lookup already solves it exactly", "The answer is needed in a few milliseconds"], [0, 2], "Unstructured language input and cheap review fit the strengths and the risk profile of a model. The other options describe cases where code or lookups are better.", null),
    S("ds010", "design", "Stakeholders say the new assistant must \"reduce handling time\". What should be done before building?", ["Measure current handling time by case type, then agree a target and tracking", "Build the assistant first and calculate the saving from the first month of data", "Set the target at the figure quoted in the vendor's marketing material", "Treat reduced handling time as proven if agents say they like the tool"], 0, "A baseline and a defined metric are needed to show any improvement. Without them, later estimates and opinions have nothing to be compared with.", null),
    S("ds011", "design", "A pipeline sends documents to Claude and writes results to a case system. Adjusters often rewrite the output, but the team never sees the changes. What is the main architectural gap?", ["The model is too small, so output quality cannot improve", "There is no feedback loop capturing human corrections", "The output stage lacks streaming, so adjusters wait for edits", "The input stage should convert every document to plain text first"], 1, "Without capturing corrections, the team cannot measure quality or learn from errors. Model size, streaming and conversion do not address the missing loop.", null),
    S("ds012", "design", "Claim attachments arrive as PDFs, phone photos and occasional corrupt files. Where should format and size problems be handled?", ["In the system prompt, by telling Claude to ignore damaged files", "By the adjuster, once the failure message finally reaches the review queue for the case", "In an input stage that validates files and routes bad ones to manual handling", "At output time, by discarding any result that looks unusual"], 2, "Input validation catches malformed items before they waste calls or corrupt later stages, and gives a clean manual path.", "scDS1"),
    S("ds013", "design", "A downstream system requires fixed fields, such as a severity class from a closed list. Which output design is best?", ["Request JSON in the prompt and write the text straight into the system", "Define a schema, request structured output, and validate the values in code before writing", "Parse the reply with a regular expression and default missing fields to blank", "Ask a second Claude call to check that the first reply looks correct"], 1, "A schema gives structure, and code-side validation of values gives a preventive gate before the system of record is touched.", null),
    M("ds014", "design", 2, "Which TWO practices make a feedback loop effective for a production Claude solution?", ["Log inputs, outputs and prompt and model versions for each case", "Delete stored outputs after a day so that logs stay small", "Sample reviewed cases and human corrections into the evaluation set", "Retrain the model weights nightly on whichever cases failed", "Collect user ratings but keep them separate from engineering work, as a rule"], [0, 2], "Versioned logs make regressions traceable, and reviewed cases feed evaluation. Nightly retraining of model weights on failures is not a practical feedback mechanism, and separating feedback from engineering wastes it.", null),
    S("ds015", "design", "Legal requires that every claim decision can be explained months later. Which design supports this best?", ["Keep only the final decision letter, since it states the outcome and the reasons", "Store a summary written by the adjuster after the decision was made, for each case", "Rely on the model provider's own logs as the record of what happened to a case", "Persist inputs, retrieved passages, intermediate outputs and versions"], 3, "A complete, versioned trail lets the case be reconstructed. A letter or a later summary cannot show what the system saw and did.", "scDS1"),
    S("ds016", "design", "In a five-step pipeline, step four sometimes fails on a transient error. What is the soundest design?", ["Make steps idempotent, retry step four with backoff, and queue repeat failures", "Restart the whole pipeline from step one whenever any single step fails, then continue", "Skip step four on failure and continue so the case still completes", "Add a longer system prompt so that step four stops failing"], 0, "Idempotent steps with bounded retries and a manual path keep cases safe and cheap to recover.", null),
    S("ds017", "design", "A support assistant must remember a customer's earlier conversations across sessions. Where does that memory belong?", ["Inside the model, which retains earlier conversations by default between separate sessions", "In the application, which stores customer history and supplies the relevant context on each call", "In the system prompt, edited by hand each time the customer returns for a new session", "In the API provider's session, which persists between separate requests from the same app"], 1, "The Messages API is stateless, so the application owns storage and decides what context to send each time.", null),
    S("ds018", "design", "A drafting service sometimes proposes refunds above policy limits. Which control is strongest?", ["Weekly sampling of past refunds to find any that exceeded limits", "A dashboard alert when the average refund amount rises", "A validation gate that blocks any refund above the limit", "A prompt line asking the model to respect the refund limit"], 2, "A preventive gate stops the error before it happens. Sampling and alerts detect it afterwards, and a prompt line is not enforcement.", null),
    S("ds019", "design", "A user-facing search feature adds a slow enrichment step that is not needed for the first answer. How should it be placed?", ["Before the answer, so the response is complete when it first appears", "Inside the same prompt, so that Claude does both jobs in a single call", "Run it asynchronously and update the result when it completes", "Remove enrichment, as latency can never be traded for completeness"], 2, "Moving non-essential work off the critical path protects perceived latency while keeping the added value.", null),
    S("ds020", "design", "Quality drops in a four-stage pipeline, and nobody can say which stage is responsible. What would have prevented this?", ["Switching every stage to the largest available model", "A trace ID with per-stage logging and evaluation", "Longer instructions in each stage's prompt", "Removing some of the user-facing features"], 1, "Tracing and per-stage evaluation locate where degradation begins. Bigger models and longer prompts change cost but not visibility.", null),
    S("ds021", "design", "A team needs answers to product questions drawn from a documentation set. The question flow is simple. Which pattern is the best starting point?", ["A single model call augmented with retrieval of the relevant documents", "An autonomous agent that plans its own search strategy", "A multi-agent system with one agent per document folder", "An evaluator-optimizer loop that rewrites every answer twice"], 0, "A single augmented call is the simplest design that meets the need, and complexity should be added only when measurements justify it.", null),
    S("ds022", "design", "Which statement best distinguishes a workflow from an agent?", ["A workflow uses tools, whereas an agent does not use them at all", "In a workflow the model chooses its own steps, whereas an agent follows a script", "In a workflow code defines the path, whereas an agent directs its own steps", "A workflow always makes one model call, whereas an agent makes many"], 2, "The distinction is who controls the flow: predefined code paths for workflows, the model for agents.", null),
    S("ds023", "design", "The claims process has fixed stages: extract, validate against policy, classify severity, draft the letter. Which pattern fits best?", ["A prompt chain with a gate between each stage", "A fully autonomous agent choosing its own order of work", "Voting across five parallel runs of the whole task", "A single prompt that is asked to do all four stages at once"], 0, "Fixed, cleanly separable steps suit chaining, with gates catching errors before they travel downstream.", "scDS1"),
    S("ds024", "design", "Incoming claims range from simple glass breakage to complex liability disputes, and the insurer wants low cost on the simple ones. Which design best supports that?", ["Send every claim to the largest model, to keep the design uniform", "Process every claim through the same long chain of steps", "Let adjusters choose manually which model each claim should use, based on judgement", "Classify each claim on arrival and route it to a path sized to its complexity"], 3, "Routing matches effort to need, so simple cases cost less and complex ones still get stronger handling.", "scDS1"),
    S("ds025", "design", "A contract review must check one document for compliance issues, personal data and tone, and the three checks do not depend on each other. What is the best design?", ["Run the three checks sequentially, so each can use the previous result", "Run the three checks in parallel as independent calls and merge the findings", "Combine all checks into one prompt to save on the number of calls", "Run one check and skip the others unless the first finds a problem"], 1, "Independent subtasks suit sectioning, which cuts latency and gives each call a focused job.", null),
    M("ds026", "design", 2, "Which TWO conditions favour an agent over a fixed workflow?", ["The number and order of steps cannot be predicted from the input", "Every case follows the same three steps in a fixed order each time", "Strict cost predictability matters more than any flexibility", "The task is open-ended and extra latency and cost are acceptable", "A single call with retrieval already meets the stated quality bar"], [0, 3], "Agents earn their cost when the path is unpredictable and the task justifies extra latency and spend. Fixed paths, strict cost control and good single-call results favour simpler designs.", null),
    S("ds027", "design", "A team drafts marketing copy and wants an evaluator-optimizer loop. When is that pattern most justified?", ["When copy is judged purely by taste, with no stated criteria at all", "When first drafts already meet the brand guide without any revision", "When speed to publish matters more than the quality of the copy", "When explicit criteria exist and each round measurably helps"], 3, "The pattern needs clear evaluation criteria and a demonstrated gain per iteration; otherwise it only adds cost and delay.", null),
    S("ds028", "design", "An executive asks for an agent to run a three-step, rarely changing approval process because agents are \"the future\". What should the architect recommend?", ["Build the agent anyway, to avoid rework when requirements grow", "Implement a simple workflow or direct call, and revisit if needed", "Build a multi-agent system now, because it will be easier to extend later", "Postpone the project until agents are more capable"], 1, "Use the simplest design that meets the requirement. Predictable steps are cheaper, faster and easier to test as a workflow.", null),
    S("ds029", "design", "An agent investigating customer problems sometimes keeps calling tools without converging. Which design change helps most?", ["Remove all tools, so that it must answer from memory without investigating anything", "Let it run longer, since more steps will eventually produce an answer on their own", "Add a step limit, clear stopping conditions and a human checkpoint for consequential actions", "Replace the system prompt with a shorter one that gives it fewer instructions to follow"], 2, "Agents need bounded loops, clear stopping criteria and human oversight. More steps without bounds increases cost and risk.", null),
    S("ds030", "design", "Ninety percent of support tickets follow known patterns, while ten percent need open-ended investigation across systems. Which architecture is best?", ["A workflow for the common cases that escalates hard cases to an agent", "One agent for every ticket, to avoid having to maintain two separate paths", "A workflow for all tickets, with hard ones left unanswered", "A multi-agent system for every ticket, for consistent handling"], 0, "A hybrid keeps predictability and low cost for the bulk and uses agent flexibility only where it pays off.", null),
    S("ds031", "design", "Some claims involve liability disputes where the investigation path cannot be predicted. How should the insurer handle them in the design?", ["Force them through the same fixed chain as the simple claims in the queue", "Use an agent with bounded tools, a step limit and adjuster oversight", "Reject them from automation and give no assistance at all to the adjusters", "Let an agent settle them automatically to save adjuster time on each case"], 1, "Unpredictable cases may justify an agent, but bounded and supervised, given payout risk and the audit requirement.", "scDS1"),
    M("ds032", "design", 2, "Which TWO are real costs of replacing a workflow with an autonomous agent?", ["A guarantee that quality will rise on every single task", "Lower token use per task, because the model plans its own work", "Higher and less predictable latency and cost per task", "Simpler regression testing, since fewer paths exist", "Errors that can compound over many self-directed steps"], [2, 4], "Agents trade predictability for flexibility: latency and cost vary, and mistakes can accumulate across steps. Testing is harder, not easier.", null),
    S("ds033", "design", "Why is an orchestrator with parallel subagents a good fit for the broad competitor-mapping requests?", ["It guarantees lower total token use than one agent handling the whole request", "It removes the need to write clear instructions for each subtask the lead delegates", "It guarantees that sources will never contradict one another in the final report", "It lets independent search directions run in parallel, each in its own context"], 3, "Broad, parallelisable investigations benefit from separate contexts and concurrent exploration, at the price of higher token use.", "scDS2"),
    S("ds034", "design", "The lead agent delegates a search to a subagent with the instruction \"research the sector\". Two subagents duplicate each other's work. What is the best fix?", ["Give each subagent a specific objective, output format and clear boundaries", "Run the subagents one after another so duplicates are noticed", "Use a longer lead system prompt and keep delegation prompts short", "Let subagents read the lead's full conversation so they can infer the overall goal"], 0, "Subagents work from the brief they receive, so specific objectives and boundaries prevent overlap. Sequencing only hides the cause.", "scDS2"),
    S("ds035", "design", "Subagents return long raw transcripts and the lead agent's context fills up. Which change is most appropriate?", ["Increase the number of subagents so each returns less", "Have subagents return condensed, cited findings", "Drop the source references to shorten each result", "Merge the subagents' work back into a single agent"], 1, "Condensed, cited findings protect the lead's context while keeping claims traceable.", "scDS2"),
    S("ds036", "design", "Management worries about token spend. Which design response is soundest for the research platform?", ["Always run the full multi-agent process for every request, to keep results consistent", "Remove subagents entirely and accept shallower reports", "Scale effort to request complexity, using one agent for simple fact checks", "Cap every report at a fixed word count, whatever the question"], 2, "Multi-agent runs cost many times a single chat, so effort should match the value and complexity of the request.", "scDS2"),
    M("ds037", "design", 2, "Which TWO signals suggest a multi-agent design is a poor fit for a task?", ["Steps are tightly interdependent and need shared context throughout", "The work splits into independent search directions", "The task is high value and exceeds one context window", "The task is low value, so extra token cost is hard to justify", "Sources can be explored in parallel without coordination"], [0, 3], "Tight interdependence and low value both undermine the case. Independent, parallel, high-value work is where the pattern pays.", "scDS2"),
    S("ds038", "design", "A team wants the lead agent to decide how many subagents to spawn. What is the most robust way to guide it?", ["Leave it entirely to the model with no guidance", "Embed explicit guidance on effort by query type in the lead prompt", "Always spawn the maximum number of subagents the platform allows, for every request", "Choose a random number each time, to avoid bias"], 1, "Explicit guidance on scaling effort to complexity avoids both over-spending on simple questions and under-investing in hard ones.", "scDS2"),
    S("ds039", "design", "Two subagents return conflicting figures for a competitor's market share. What should the design do?", ["Average the two figures and report the mean as the best available estimate", "Choose whichever figure came from the first subagent to finish its search", "Hide the conflict so that the final report reads smoothly for the client team", "Have the lead weigh sources and report the conflict with citations"], 3, "Traceability matters. A lead that weighs sources and discloses conflicts, or checks further, produces a defensible report.", "scDS2"),
    S("ds040", "design", "A standard subagent is asked to continue \"the analysis we discussed earlier\". Why is it likely to fail?", ["Subagents cannot use tools that the lead used", "A subagent sees only the prompt it receives, not the lead's conversation", "Subagents only accept prompts that are shorter than a single short sentence", "Subagents cannot return results to the lead"], 1, "Subagents have fresh, isolated contexts. The delegation prompt is the main channel into them, so it must contain what they need.", null),
    S("ds041", "design", "One subagent in a parallel run times out. What is the best orchestration behaviour?", ["Fail the whole run, because a partial report is never acceptable to readers", "Ignore the gap and present the report as complete without any caveat", "Retry the subtask, then deliver partial results with the gap stated", "Restart every subagent from the beginning and rerun the whole job"], 2, "Orchestration should tolerate partial failure with bounded retries and honest reporting.", null),
    S("ds042", "design", "A subagent exists only to review drafts for factual errors. Which tool configuration fits best?", ["Full write access to the document store and the shell", "Read-only tools needed for the review and nothing more", "All tools of the lead agent, in case the review needs them", "No tools, with all content pasted into the prompt every time"], 1, "Least privilege limits the damage from a mistake or injected content, and a reviewer has no need to write.", null),
    M("ds043", "design", 2, "Which TWO practices help a long-running multi-agent task survive context limits?", ["Keep the full history of every subagent in the lead's context window", "Persist the plan and key findings outside the context", "Store large outputs as artifacts and return references", "Avoid writing a plan so that the model stays more flexible as it goes", "Restart from scratch whenever context nears its limit, then repeat the work"], [1, 2], "External plans and artifact references preserve state without bloating the lead's context.", null),
    S("ds044", "design", "A procurement summary must be produced for a very large set of supplier documents. Which decomposition is best?", ["Summarise each chunk independently, then combine the partial summaries", "Paste every document into one prompt regardless of size", "Summarise only the first document and extrapolate", "Ask an agent to read the files one at a time with no overall plan to follow"], 0, "A map-then-reduce shape handles corpora larger than one context and allows parallel work.", null),
    S("ds045", "design", "A team splits a prompt that extracts, judges and drafts into three prompts, with a validation check between them. What is the main benefit?", ["It always reduces the total number of tokens used across the run", "It removes the need for evaluation data at each separate stage", "Each stage has a simpler job and can be tested on its own", "It guarantees that the final output is correct for every input"], 2, "Decomposition gives simpler tasks, intermediate checks and clearer diagnosis, though not guaranteed correctness or lower cost.", null),
    S("ds046", "design", "A pipeline has fourteen tiny steps. Latency is high and errors propagate. What is the best next step?", ["Add more steps so that each does even less", "Add retries to every step without changing the overall structure of the chain", "Replace the pipeline with one prompt doing everything", "Merge steps to boundaries where each can still be tested independently"], 3, "Granularity is a trade-off. Aim for steps that are independently testable without paying for needless hand-offs.", null),
    S("ds047", "design", "A task decomposes into five subtasks where B and C need A, D needs B and C, and E is independent. What is the best execution plan?", ["Run all five concurrently and merge the results at the end", "Run all five sequentially in alphabetical order of their names", "Run A, then B and C together, then D, with E in parallel", "Run only the ones needed by D, and ignore E altogether as unnecessary"], 2, "Mapping dependencies lets independent branches run together while dependent steps wait for validated upstream output.", null),
    S("ds048", "design", "A research request needs different subtasks depending on what the first search finds, so they cannot be listed up front. Which decomposition suits it?", ["A fixed prompt chain with five predefined steps, run in order every time", "A voting workflow that repeats the same prompt several times and takes the majority", "A single-call workflow with no tools or any planning of the work", "A dynamic orchestrator-worker design that creates subtasks from the input"], 3, "Subtasks that depend on the input call for dynamic decomposition. Static chains suit subtasks known in advance.", null),
    M("ds049", "design", 2, "Which TWO observations suggest a task should be decomposed?", ["One prompt gives inconsistent results because it mixes different jobs", "Output is already stable and fully meets the quality bar", "Independent parts could run concurrently to cut latency", "The task has one trivial step", "Adding more steps would require no extra evaluation"], [0, 2], "Mixed jobs in one prompt and independent parts are classic reasons to decompose. Stable, simple tasks do not need it.", null),
    S("ds050", "design", "End-to-end quality of a four-step chain is poor, but the team has only an end-to-end test. What should they do first?", ["Rewrite all four prompts at the same time", "Switch to a larger model and re-run the same end-to-end test to compare", "Build per-stage evaluation cases to locate where quality is lost", "Add a fifth step to correct the earlier ones"], 2, "Errors compound across steps, so stage-level evaluation is needed to find the real source.", null),
    S("ds051", "design", "An autonomous agent performs 30 dependent steps, each about 95 percent reliable. Why is end-to-end reliability a concern?", ["Errors compound across dependent steps, so reliability falls", "Agents skip accuracy checks, so each step is effectively unreliable", "Reliability only matters on the last step, so earlier errors fade away", "Step count affects cost only, and has no bearing on end-to-end quality"], 0, "Compounding errors over many steps reduce overall reliability, which argues for fewer steps, gates and checkpoints.", null),
    S("ds052", "design", "A company wants to know which business pillar a Claude-based tool serves when it lets analysts finish reports in hours instead of days. Which fits best?", ["Compliance, measured by the number of audit findings raised", "Availability SLA, measured as uptime of the reporting service", "Transformation only, since reports are now automated end to end by the tool", "Efficiency and productivity, measured as time and output per analyst"], 3, "Time saved per report and more output per person are efficiency and productivity measures.", null),
    S("ds053", "design", "A support team routes easy tickets to a smaller model and complex ones to a larger model. Which pillar is that decision mainly serving?", ["Cost and performance, by matching model size to difficulty", "Brand identity, through one consistent voice in all replies", "Legal defensibility, through reduced logging of customer data", "Transformation, through a new product line for premium clients"], 0, "Routing by difficulty trades off cost and speed against quality at the level of each request.", null),
    S("ds054", "design", "A service allows small businesses to get tailored contract reviews that were previously too expensive to offer. Which pillar describes this best?", ["Cost reduction, because the same reviews now cost less to produce", "Performance SLA, because customers receive responses faster", "Developer productivity, since engineers ship the review feature faster", "Transformation, since it enables a service that was previously uneconomic"], 3, "Enabling a service that was not viable before is transformation, rather than doing the same work more cheaply.", null),
    S("ds055", "design", "A nightly backlog of 100,000 low-priority documents must be classified by morning. Which approach best balances cost and the deadline?", ["Use the batch API, started early enough to meet the deadline", "Process them one by one through the interactive API during the working day", "Run each one through a multi-agent system for the best possible quality", "Leave them until a user asks about a specific document, then classify it"], 0, "Work with nobody waiting suits asynchronous batch processing, which is aimed at lower cost rather than immediate latency.", null),
    S("ds056", "design", "The insurer promises customers a status reply in a few seconds in the portal. Which processing path should serve it?", ["Overnight batch processing, to reduce the cost of each answer", "A manual email to the customer once an adjuster has checked", "A multi-agent investigation for each question, to maximise accuracy and depth", "A synchronous low-latency path, possibly streaming and a smaller model"], 3, "An interactive latency commitment needs a synchronous path. Batch is asynchronous and unsuited to second-level SLAs.", "scDS1"),
    M("ds057", "design", 2, "Which TWO items belong in the business case for a Claude-based solution?", ["A baseline measure and the measured improvement against it", "Only the token cost, since other costs are small", "Model benchmark rankings as a proxy for value", "A promise of the highest quality, lowest cost and lowest latency together", "The full cost, including human review, evaluation and monitoring"], [0, 4], "A credible case compares measured impact with a baseline and counts total cost of ownership.", null),
    S("ds058", "design", "A stakeholder wants the highest possible accuracy at the lowest cost and latency. What should the architect do?", ["Agree to all three and choose the largest model available", "Reject the request as unreasonable and ask for revised goals", "Show measured trade-offs from a prototype and agree priorities", "Promise to deliver all three after launch, once usage data is available"], 2, "Competing qualities need ranking and evidence. A prototype shows what each option really delivers.", null),
    S("ds059", "design", "Three use cases are proposed. Which prioritisation is most defensible?", ["Rank by value and feasibility, and mark quick wins", "Rank by which team asked first, as that shows the strongest demand", "Rank by the size of the largest model that each case could use", "Start with the most technically impressive one, to build confidence"], 0, "Value and feasibility prioritisation, with quick wins identified, balances impact and risk.", null),
    S("ds060", "design", "Is the multi-agent research platform economically justified, and how should the consultancy decide?", ["No, because multi-agent runs cost more than analyst time", "Yes, as long as the model chosen is large enough for the work", "Decide after counting only the tokens used on the first report generated", "Compare cost per report, with review, against analyst hours saved"], 3, "High-value work can justify heavy token use, but the case rests on comparing total cost per report with the measured analyst time saved.", "scDS2")
  );

  Object.assign(window.CCA.why, {
    "ds001": ["A capable model does not define the problem, so the pilot has no target to judge against.", "Open-ended feedback on a broad prompt gives impressions, not a measurable outcome.", "A concrete task, an outcome and a measure give the project a testable scope before any design choice.", "A framework choice is premature and adds complexity before the problem is understood."],
    "ds002": ["A calculation tool gives exact results and Claude supplies the natural-language explanation.", "Worked examples improve behaviour but do not make language-model arithmetic exact.", "Repeating the calculation and voting raises cost and still gives no guarantee of exactness.", "A bigger model may help but does not make exact calculation reliable, and code does it for free."],
    "ds003": ["A threshold is an exact rule, so ordinary code applies it cheaply and repeatably.", "Forms vary and include free text and scans, which is where language understanding adds the most value.", "Matching a number to a record is a lookup that code does exactly.", "Routing by threshold is a deterministic rule, not a language task."],
    "ds004": ["Summarising a narrative is a language task where Claude helps and a human can review.", "The threshold is an exact, auditable rule, so it belongs in code rather than model judgement.", "A first draft of a letter suits Claude, with review by a person.", "Final approval above the threshold is a human accountability that the scenario reserves for adjusters.", "Extracting a date from a form is a task for the model with validation, not a rule."],
    "ds005": ["Training a bespoke model is heavy, and policies change, so retrieval is simpler and more current.", "Web access adds risk and irrelevant sources when the answers are in internal documents.", "Answering from memory risks inventing policy, and a disclaimer does not make it reliable.", "Grounding in the policy library with citations solves the finding problem and lets staff verify answers."],
    "ds006": ["Testing a prototype against a rubric on representative real cases gives evidence of feasibility and seeds an evaluation set.", "A demo uses chosen examples and says little about performance on real, varied cases.", "Waiting does not test this use case, and new models still need evaluation.", "A vendor statement is not evidence about your data and your quality bar."],
    "ds007": ["High pilot accuracy does not remove the legal and ethical need for human oversight in a regulated decision.", "A human decision owner with early Legal and Compliance involvement fits a high-impact decision, with Claude assisting rather than deciding.", "A monthly audit of declines is a detective control and does not satisfy oversight for each decision.", "Two agreeing calls can both be wrong and do not create accountability."],
    "ds008": ["Topic detection in free text is a classic language task.", "Drafting for human review tolerates variation, which suits a model.", "An identical checksum result each time is exact computation, which code does and a model cannot guarantee.", "Summarising threads is a language task where some variation is acceptable."],
    "ds009": ["Unstructured language is where models offer the most value over conventional code.", "Bit-identical output is a requirement code can meet and a model cannot guarantee.", "Cheap review reduces the risk of occasional errors, so it fits a model-based step.", "If a rule already solves it exactly, code is cheaper and more reliable.", "Millisecond latency generally rules out a model call."],
    "ds010": ["A baseline and a defined target and tracking method are needed to show any gain.", "Without a prior baseline, the later figure cannot be compared with anything.", "Marketing figures say nothing about this organisation's own process.", "Agent preference is an opinion, not a measure of handling time."],
    "ds011": ["Model size does not create a way to learn from corrections that are not captured.", "Capturing and reviewing human edits into evaluation data closes the loop so quality can be measured and improved.", "Streaming changes perceived latency, not learning from corrections.", "Plain-text conversion is an input issue and does not address missing feedback."],
    "ds012": ["A prompt cannot reliably fix corrupt files and wastes calls on inputs that should be filtered.", "Adjusters should not discover technical failures late in the queue.", "An input stage validates, normalises and sends bad items to manual handling before they affect later stages.", "Discarding at output time spends calls on bad inputs and may drop valid results."],
    "ds013": ["Asking for JSON in the prompt alone does not guarantee the shape or valid values, and unchecked writes are risky.", "A schema with structured output plus code validation gives a preventive gate before the system of record is updated.", "Regular-expression parsing is brittle, and silent defaults hide errors.", "A second model call adds cost and is still not a deterministic check."],
    "ds014": ["Versioned logs make regressions and failures traceable to a prompt or model change.", "Short retention removes the evidence needed to investigate and evaluate.", "Reviewed cases and corrections become evaluation data that measure and guide improvement.", "Nightly retraining of weights on failed cases is not a practical or safe feedback mechanism compared with improving prompts, tools and evaluation data.", "Isolated ratings do not feed into engineering and waste the signal."],
    "ds015": ["A letter states the outcome but not what the system saw or did, so the decision cannot be reconstructed.", "A later summary reflects memory and interpretation, not the actual record.", "The provider's logs are not your audit trail and may not cover your intermediate data.", "Storing inputs, retrieved passages, intermediate outputs and versions allows the case to be reconstructed on demand."],
    "ds016": ["Idempotent steps with bounded retries and a manual queue recover cheaply and safely.", "Restarting everything wastes cost and may repeat side effects.", "Skipping a step silently can produce an incorrect or incomplete result.", "A longer prompt does not fix transient infrastructure errors."],
    "ds017": ["The model keeps no memory across separate API calls by default.", "The application stores history and supplies relevant context, because the API is stateless.", "Hand-editing a system prompt does not scale and is error prone.", "The API does not hold a persistent session between requests."],
    "ds018": ["Sampling finds problems after refunds have been issued.", "An alert is also detective and shows only an average shift, not the single bad refund.", "A gate in code blocks the over-limit refund before it is issued, which is a preventive control.", "A prompt instruction can be ignored and is not enforcement."],
    "ds019": ["Placing the slow step first delays every response.", "Putting both jobs into one prompt does not remove the slow dependency.", "Running enrichment asynchronously keeps the first answer fast and adds the extra value later.", "Latency and completeness can be traded off, so removing enrichment entirely is unnecessary."],
    "ds020": ["A bigger model does not show which stage is at fault.", "A trace ID with per-stage logs and evaluations makes the failing stage identifiable.", "More prompt text changes behaviour but gives no visibility.", "Removing features does not locate the degraded stage."],
    "ds021": ["A single call with retrieval is the simplest design meeting the need and can be extended if measurements justify it.", "An agent adds latency and cost for a flow that does not need its flexibility.", "Many agents add coordination overhead without a clear benefit.", "Rewriting each answer twice adds cost without criteria showing it helps."],
    "ds022": ["Both workflows and agents can use tools, so tool use does not distinguish them.", "The roles are reversed: in a workflow, code drives the path.", "Workflows follow predefined code paths, while agents direct their own process and tool use.", "Neither the number of calls nor a single call defines the difference."],
    "ds023": ["Fixed, separable stages suit a chain, and gates prevent errors from travelling downstream.", "An agent choosing its own order is unnecessary for a known sequence.", "Voting over the whole task multiplies cost and does not address stage structure.", "One prompt for four jobs is harder to test and gives less consistent results."],
    "ds024": ["Sending everything to the largest model raises cost for simple cases.", "A uniform long chain wastes effort on easy claims.", "Manual model selection adds delay and inconsistency.", "Classifying on arrival and routing by complexity matches effort to need and controls cost."],
    "ds025": ["Sequencing independent checks adds latency without any benefit.", "Independent checks can run in parallel, which reduces latency and gives each call a focused job.", "A combined prompt makes each check less focused and harder to evaluate.", "Skipping checks conditionally risks missing issues the other checks would have found."],
    "ds026": ["When the path cannot be predicted, a fixed workflow cannot cover it and an agent can adapt.", "A fixed three-step sequence is the textbook case for a workflow.", "Strict cost predictability favours a workflow, since agent cost varies.", "Open-ended problems justify agent flexibility if latency and cost are acceptable.", "If a single call already works, the agent adds only cost."],
    "ds027": ["Taste without stated criteria gives the evaluator nothing to optimise against.", "If drafts already meet the guide, extra rounds add cost only.", "Extra rounds add latency, so this is the opposite of when to use the loop.", "Clear criteria and a measurable gain per round are what justify the pattern."],
    "ds028": ["Building an agent for an unchanging process adds cost, variability and testing burden without need.", "A simple workflow is predictable and cheap, and can be revisited if evidence calls for an agent.", "Multi-agent adds even more overhead for a three-step process.", "Postponing is unnecessary because a workflow solves the problem today."],
    "ds029": ["Removing tools prevents the agent from doing the investigation at all.", "Unbounded additional steps raise cost and risk without a guarantee of convergence.", "Step limits, stopping conditions and checkpoints bound the loop and protect against consequential errors.", "A shorter prompt does not change the unbounded nature of the loop."],
    "ds030": ["A hybrid gives low cost and predictability for the bulk, and agent flexibility where needed.", "One agent for everything makes the common case slower and costlier than necessary.", "Leaving hard tickets unanswered fails the users who most need help.", "Multi-agent for all tickets over-engineers the common cases."],
    "ds031": ["A fixed chain cannot handle a path that is unpredictable.", "A bounded, supervised agent fits an unpredictable investigation while keeping payout risk and audit needs in check.", "Excluding them gives no value where adjusters could use help.", "Automatic settlement removes human approval where risk is highest."],
    "ds032": ["Agents give flexibility, not a guarantee of better quality.", "Agent runs typically use more tokens, not fewer.", "Latency and cost are higher and vary per task, which is a real cost of agents.", "Testing is harder because there are many possible paths.", "Mistakes can accumulate over many self-directed steps."],
    "ds033": ["Multi-agent designs use more tokens than a single agent, not fewer.", "Each subtask still needs clear instructions from the lead.", "Contradictions between sources can still occur and must be handled.", "Parallel subagents with separate contexts suit broad, independent search directions."],
    "ds034": ["Specific objectives, output formats, tool guidance and boundaries stop subagents from overlapping or leaving gaps.", "Running sequentially hides the problem and loses the benefit of parallelism.", "The delegation prompt is what the subagent works from, so keeping it short is the problem.", "Subagents start from the prompt they are given, and the lead's whole conversation is not their input."],
    "ds035": ["More subagents do not reduce the volume returned by each one.", "Condensed findings with source references protect the lead's context and keep claims traceable.", "Removing sources damages traceability, which management requires.", "Merging into one agent loses the benefit of parallel, separate contexts."],
    "ds036": ["Running the full process every time wastes tokens on simple questions.", "Removing subagents lowers depth for the broad requests that need it.", "Scaling effort to complexity keeps spend aligned with value and uses a single agent where enough.", "A fixed word count does not control token spend and may cut needed detail."],
    "ds037": ["Tightly interdependent steps need shared context, which isolated subagents lack.", "Independent search directions are a good fit for parallel agents.", "High-value work that exceeds one context window is where the pattern pays off.", "If the task is low value, the multi-agent token cost is hard to justify.", "Parallel exploration without coordination suits the pattern."],
    "ds038": ["No guidance leads to over-spending on simple queries or under-investing in hard ones.", "Explicit effort guidance in the prompt scales subagent use to the complexity of the query.", "Always maximum wastes tokens on simple queries.", "Random choices ignore the complexity of the request."],
    "ds039": ["Averaging contradictory figures can create a number no source supports.", "Speed of completion says nothing about reliability.", "Hiding a conflict undermines the traceability the business requires.", "Weighing source quality and disclosing or verifying the conflict keeps the report defensible."],
    "ds040": ["Tool availability is configurable and unrelated to this failure.", "A subagent begins with a fresh context and sees only the prompt it was given.", "Subagents accept prompts of any reasonable length.", "Subagents return their results to the lead agent."],
    "ds041": ["Failing the whole run discards useful work and is unnecessarily brittle.", "Presenting an incomplete result as complete misleads the reader.", "A bounded retry followed by an honest partial report is robust.", "Restarting everything wastes cost and time."],
    "ds042": ["Write access is unnecessary and raises risk for a reviewer.", "Read-only tools limit damage from errors or injected content while allowing the review.", "Inheriting every tool breaks least privilege.", "Pasting everything into the prompt each time is inefficient and still gives no reason for tool access."],
    "ds043": ["Keeping every subagent's full history fills the lead's context quickly.", "Persisting the plan and key findings outside the context preserves state across limits.", "Artifacts and references keep large outputs out of the lead's context.", "Without a plan, progress can be lost when context is trimmed.", "Restarting discards finished work and wastes cost."],
    "ds044": ["Map then reduce handles corpora larger than a single context and can run in parallel.", "A single prompt cannot hold unbounded input.", "Extrapolating from one document ignores the rest.", "Reading with no plan is slow and unfocused."],
    "ds045": ["A token reduction is not guaranteed and is not the main benefit.", "Evaluation data is still needed for each stage.", "Simpler jobs per stage give stage-level testing and debugging.", "Decomposition does not guarantee correctness."],
    "ds046": ["More steps worsen latency and error propagation.", "Retries do not fix the structural overhead.", "One prompt doing everything brings back inconsistency and poor diagnosability.", "Merging to independently testable boundaries restores a balance of focus and overhead."],
    "ds047": ["Running everything concurrently ignores dependencies.", "Sequential alphabetical order wastes time on independent work.", "Following the dependency graph runs independent branches in parallel and gates dependent steps.", "Ignoring E drops needed work."],
    "ds048": ["A fixed chain cannot adapt to subtasks that depend on what the search finds.", "Voting repeats the same task and does not decompose it.", "A no-tool single call cannot search.", "Dynamic orchestrator-worker decomposition creates subtasks from the input as it arrives."],
    "ds049": ["A prompt mixing different jobs is a classic sign that decomposition would help.", "Stable, adequate output gives no reason to add complexity.", "Independent parts running concurrently can cut latency.", "A single trivial step does not need decomposing.", "More steps always add evaluation needs, so this premise is wrong."],
    "ds050": ["Rewriting everything at once makes it impossible to see what helped.", "A larger model may not fix a weak stage and hides the diagnosis.", "Stage-level evaluation cases show where quality is lost so the fix can be targeted.", "An extra step adds more places for errors."],
    "ds051": ["Errors compound across dependent steps, so overall reliability falls as the step count grows, which argues for fewer steps, gates and checkpoints.", "Nothing suggests agents skip checks; the concern is the accumulation of ordinary per-step errors.", "Earlier errors propagate into later steps instead of fading away.", "Step count affects reliability as well as cost, because errors accumulate."],
    "ds052": ["Compliance is not what this tool is mainly serving.", "An availability SLA concerns uptime and not report time.", "The work is accelerated rather than made newly possible, so it is not mainly transformation.", "Time saved per report and output per analyst are efficiency and productivity measures."],
    "ds053": ["Matching model size to difficulty trades cost and speed against quality per request.", "Brand voice is not the aim of routing by difficulty.", "Reduced logging would harm defensibility and is not what routing does.", "Routing does not create a new product line."],
    "ds054": ["Cost reduction describes doing existing work cheaper, not creating a new service.", "An SLA concerns commitments such as latency, not what the service enables.", "Developer productivity concerns engineering tools, not a customer service.", "Enabling a previously uneconomic service is transformation."],
    "ds055": ["Asynchronous batch processing suits work nobody waits for and is aimed at lower cost, if started with margin before the deadline.", "Interactive processing at peak is more expensive than needed for low-priority work.", "Multi-agent runs add cost with no benefit for simple classification.", "Waiting until asked leaves the morning deadline unmet."],
    "ds056": ["Batch processing is asynchronous and cannot meet a few-second promise.", "Manual email misses the stated few-second portal promise.", "A multi-agent investigation adds latency and cost for a status query.", "A synchronous, low-latency path matches an interactive latency commitment."],
    "ds057": ["A baseline with measured improvement is the basis of a credible case.", "Other costs such as review and monitoring are not small and must be counted.", "Benchmarks are not a measure of business value.", "Promising all three together without measurement is not credible.", "Total cost, including review, evaluation and monitoring, gives an honest comparison."],
    "ds058": ["Choosing the largest model ignores cost and latency.", "Rejecting outright is unhelpful when a trade-off can be quantified.", "Measured trade-offs from a prototype let stakeholders rank priorities and set targets.", "Promising all three later sets up failure."],
    "ds059": ["Measured value and feasibility, with quick wins separated, is a defensible way to prioritise.", "First asked is not a measure of value or feasibility.", "Model size is not a criterion for value.", "Technical impressiveness is not a business criterion."],
    "ds060": ["Multi-agent runs cost more tokens, but high-value reports may still justify the spend.", "Model size alone does not make the economics work.", "A single report's token count ignores review and the savings side of the case.", "Comparing the full cost per report with measured analyst hours saved is how to justify or reject the investment."]
  });
})();
